{"text": " So what's the next wrong? 2100, that morning hasn't happened yet, but we can follow the evidence. Remember Prince Henry's tutor? One teacher, one child. In 1984, an American researcher, Benjamin Bloom, reported that tutored students did better than 98% of students taught in a normal class. Later studies found the effect is smaller, but it's real. The problem was always the cost. Nobody can pay a tutor for every child. Then in 2024, a World Bank pilot in Nigeria gave students an AI tutor twice a week with their teachers guiding them. After six weeks, the World Bank estimated nearly two years of typical learning. One small study, short, but it's a start. So here's one scenario, not a fact. By 2100, every child could have a tutor, not a princess poet, a patient machine, awake at any hour. In the classroom, the OECD has imagined futures where school becomes a learning hub, and even one where it fades away. But UNESCO says schools should be protected. If they didn't exist, we'd have to invent them. In 1500, a private teacher was for a prince. In 2100, it might be for everyone. And school might get a little closer to what its name first meant.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 0.96, "text": " So what's the next wrong?", "tokens": [50364, 407, 437, 311, 264, 958, 2085, 30, 50460], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11907175667265542, "compression_ratio": 1.4929078014184398, "no_speech_prob": 0.004931812174618244, "words": [{"word": " So", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.16, "probability": 0.849437415599823}, {"word": " what's", "start": 0.16, "end": 0.42, "probability": 0.9361026287078857}, {"word": " the", "start": 0.42, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9957782030105591}, {"word": " next", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.66, "probability": 0.9964410662651062}, {"word": " wrong?", "start": 0.66, "end": 0.96, "probability": 0.6917407512664795}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 2.16, "end": 4.3, "text": " 2100, that morning hasn't happened yet,", "tokens": [5